A||N||4||Willy||Is my car running backwards???||||1||
Z||000000||Willy||06-18-2001||12:27 PM||||After my car died one day and would not restart, I found the fuel pump fuse to be blown so I replaced it.  The car would start but would not last for more than a minute before blowing the fuse and redieing, redying, dieing again.  I searched the wires for shorts and found none.  On the 10th fuse it started to stay running but the whole car shook.  I turned it off and when I tried to restart it it looked like exhaust was coming from the air intake and maybe even air going in the exhaust.  Whats up with that??||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||06-18-2001||12:34 PM||||maybe have a bad fuel pump, shorting internally. 'exhaust' from intake just sounds like it was flooded/backfiring.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Willy||06-18-2001||12:49 PM||||The fuel pump works because you can hear it running. The engine is backfiring but doing it constantly.  It will maybe fire 10 times at the most but every fire a backfire.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||06-18-2001||02:39 PM||||Sounds like the fuel pump is crapping out on ya. You know it is working somewhat, because the car will start, but I'm sure its internals are shorting out, and causeing you to blow fuses.<P>FYI, even if there is a short on a hot wire, the fuse will not blow immediatly. It does take a while for a fuse to blow if the currenty draw is only little over the fuse's rating. The only way a fuse will blow quickly is if it is a direct short. So, if you had an exposed/shorting wire, the fuse would most likly blow right when you turn the car on. The fact it is blowing slowly sounds like the fuel pump itself is going out on ya.||||reg||1
Z||000004||cadaver||06-18-2001||09:53 PM||||Check the fuel pressure at the rail.||||reg||1
